# Response headers for https://meander-eoc.pages.dev.
#
# Cloudflare Pages does not read vercel.json — that file was this policy's only
# home, and it now lives in docs/legacy/ as a record. index.html carries no
# <meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy"> fallback either. So until this
# file existed the live site shipped with no CSP at all: measured, not assumed —
# `curl -sSI https://meander-eoc.pages.dev/` came back with referrer-policy and
# x-content-type-options and nothing else.
#
# Which origin owns which header is written out at the bottom of the Caddyfile.
# The short version: that file owns the API's responses, which are JSON and SSE
# and are never rendered as a document; this file owns everything that governs a
# document. They never both set a header, because no response is served by both.
#
# Every comment here sits in column 0 and between blocks, never inside one.
# Pages documents `#` at the start of a line as a comment and says nothing about
# an indented one, and an indented `# ...` inside a rule block is one plausible
# parse away from being read as a header named `#`.
#
# ── two Pages behaviours this file is shaped around ──────────────────────────
#
# 1. When a request matches several rules that set the same header name, Pages
#    JOINS the values with a comma. Setting Cache-Control under both /* and
#    /assets/* does not override, it produces
#      public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#    which is a browser's guess rather than a policy. So Cache-Control appears
#    only on disjoint patterns below, and never on /*.
#
# 2. `/` is an exact match, not a prefix, and a request for /index.html is 308'd
#    to `/` — so a rule written against /index.html only ever decorates a
#    redirect nobody consumes. The two that matter are `/` and `/*`.
#
# Limits, for anyone extending this: 100 rules, 2,000 characters per line.
#
# ── the policy ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Ported from docs/legacy/vercel.json, with two changes.
#
# connect-src names https://meander-app.duckdns.org, replacing the literal
# https://REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-RENDER-HOST.onrender.com that file still carries.
# The placeholder is honest there and would be a live hole here.
#
# script-src gains the SHA-256 of the inline theme block at index.html:41-58.
# Without it that script is blocked: the app still works, because
# src/lib/theme.js re-applies the theme once React mounts, but a dark-mode
# visitor gets a full frame of cream first and the console carries a violation.
# The hash covers the bytes between <script> and </script>, and was computed
# from the HTML Cloudflare actually served rather than from the source file.
# src/lib/csp-hash.test.js recomputes it on every run, so editing that script
# fails the suite instead of silently breaking the policy — which is what
# DEPLOY.md already asked for: "add a build check that the hash still matches,
# because it will drift the first time anyone edits it".
#
# tiles.openfreemap.org is in BOTH img-src and connect-src on purpose. The
# vector tiles, the style JSON, the glyphs and the sprite are all fetched rather
# than <img>-loaded; MapView.jsx:15 warns that a CSP which forgot connect-src
# leaves a permanently blank basemap after a 20-second timeout and no error.
#
# worker-src 'self' blob: is MapLibre, which builds its workers from blob URLs.
#
# style-src keeps 'unsafe-inline'. MapLibre writes inline styles onto the map
# container, and the <noscript> block at index.html:63 carries a style
# attribute. Removing it is real work, not a one-line tightening, and pretending
# otherwise would mean shipping a policy that blocks the map.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-rXb3GrH6fQu5rzdJqUPl87syYzPxM2UTz++hiaG3xVo='; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://tiles.openfreemap.org; font-src 'self'; worker-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self' https://tiles.openfreemap.org https://meander-app.duckdns.org; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(self), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), interest-cohort=()

# Deliberately absent: Strict-Transport-Security.
#
# Not an oversight. `.dev` is a public-suffix entry in Chromium's static preload
# list with force-https and include_subdomains, and every major browser ships a
# list derived from it — so meander-eoc.pages.dev is already unreachable over
# plaintext. The upgrade happens inside the browser, before any packet leaves,
# on the first visit by someone who has never seen the site. An HSTS response
# header is strictly weaker than that: it binds only after one successful HTTPS
# response, which is exactly the trust-on-first-use gap preloading closes.
#
# This stops being true the day this project moves to a custom domain on an
# ordinary TLD. That hostname would have no preload coverage and would want HSTS
# here — or better, at the zone level.

# Content-hashed by Vite, so the name changes whenever the bytes do. This is the
# only place `immutable` is honest.

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The shell. Pages' own default for HTML is already max-age=0, must-revalidate;
# it is written out rather than inherited because the service worker is
# cache-first over the precached shell (sw.js:168-178) and never revalidates, so
# a stale document here is pinned until the worker's bytes change — and the
# worker's version is a hash of the file *list*, which an index.html-only edit
# does not move.

/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The one file that must never be edge-cached. A stale worker keeps handing out
# a precache manifest naming assets the deploy has already replaced, and the app
# opens offline into a version that no longer exists.

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Precached by name rather than by content hash, so it has to be revalidated.

/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
